# Findings — TextMate-lineage scope naming and what Sublime deliberately changed

Sources fetched directly (raw HTML → text), not summarized from memory unless marked.

---

## 1. Sublime `scope_naming.html` — AUTHORITATIVE (vendor doc)
URL: https://www.sublimetext.com/docs/scope_naming.html

### 1a. Stated status / principles (verbatim)

&gt; "Syntax definitions and color schemes in Sublime Text interact through the use of scope names. Scopes are dotted strings, specified from least-to-most specific. For example, the `if` keyword in PHP could be specified via the scope name `keyword.control.php`."

&gt; "Sublime Text supports TextMate language grammars, and inherited its default syntaxes from various open-source bundles. The TextMate language grammar documentation provided a base set of scope names that **have been slowly expanded and changed by the community**."

&gt; "This is a **living document that attempts to document best practices** for using scope names in syntax definitions and color schemes. All of the Sublime Text default packages strive to adhere to these recommendations."

&gt; "The scopes documented below are a **recommended base set** of scope names to use when creating a syntax definition."

&gt; "It is recommended to read through the entire list at least once before writing or modifying a syntax."

**(d) language-suffix convention — verbatim:**
&gt; "In this documentation, the syntax name is omitted from the end of the dotted scope name. When writing a syntax, unless otherwise noted, **the syntax name should be the final segment of a dotted name**. For example, a control keyword in Ruby would be `keyword.control.ruby`, whereas in Python it would be `keyword.control.python`."

Sublime does *not* state the *why*; TextMate does (see §3).

**(a) when to invent a new scope — verbatim (the only guidance):**
&gt; "The names of data structures will use one of the following scopes, **or a new sub-scope of `entity.name` – this list is not exhaustive. To provide rich semantic information, use the specific terminology for a given language construct.**"
&gt; "**Avoid `entity.name.type.class` and `entity.name.type.struct` which unnecessarily nest scope labels under `type`.**"

So the doctrine is: extend at an existing root, use real domain terminology, do not stack a sub-kind under an unrelated generic level.

**(b) meta is NOT for coloring — verbatim, stated twice:**
&gt; "Meta scopes are used to scope larger sections of code or markup, generally containing multiple, more specific scopes. **These are not intended to be styled by a color scheme, but used by preferences and plugins.**"

&gt; (Color Schemes → `meta.` Colors) "**When styling scopes, resist the urge to directly style `meta` scopes. They are primarily intended to provide contextual information for preferences and plugins.**"

Plus the non-stacking rule for meta variants — verbatim:
&gt; "Each variant should be applied to a specific part, and **not stacked**. For example, `meta.function.php meta.function.parameters.php` should never occur, but instead the scopes should alternate between `meta.function.php` then `meta.function.parameters.php` and back to `meta.function.php`."
&gt; (annotations) "…there should **never be more than one `meta.annotation*` scope on the stack at any given time**."

**(c) prefer existing names so themes work — verbatim (stated from the *theme* side):**
&gt; "In general, when applying colors and styles to scopes in a color scheme, the most general form of a selector should be styled first. **High-quality syntaxes utilizing the scopes outlined in the previous section should result is good user experience for end users.**" [sic — "result is"]

&gt; (entity.name Colors) "Historically, many color schemes have provided one color for `entity.name.function` and `entity.name.type`, and often a different color for `entity.name.tag`. **This leaves new `entity.name.*` scopes un-highlighted.** Color schemes should instead specify a color for `entity.name` that will be applied to classes, types, structs, interfaces and many other data structures."

**(e) risk of overly specific scopes — verbatim:**
&gt; "The following is a recommended minimal set of scopes to highlight. **Adding extra may result in a slightly improved experience, however being too specific will result in a color scheme that often only looks good for one or two syntaxes.**"

Minimal scope coverage list (exact, in doc order):
`entity.name` · `entity.other.inherited-class` · `entity.name.section` · `entity.name.tag` · `entity.other.attribute-name` · `variable` · `variable.language` · `variable.parameter` · `variable.function` · `constant` · `constant.numeric` · `constant.language` · `constant.character.escape` · `storage.type` · `storage.modifier` · `support` · `keyword` · `keyword.control` · `keyword.operator` · `keyword.declaration` · `string` · `comment` · `invalid` · `invalid.deprecated`

INFERENCE (mine): that 24-entry list is the practical contract. A scope outside it is *decoration you cannot rely on*. It is the closest thing in the ecosystem to a "guaranteed styled" set, and note `meta.*` appears nowhere in it.

### 1b. Full top-level list + sub-scopes (exact strings, doc order)

Roots (14): `comment.` `constant.` `entity.` `invalid.` `keyword.` `markup.` `meta.` `punctuation.` `source.` `storage.` `string.` `support.` `text.` `variable.`

- **comment**: `comment.line` · `comment.block` · `comment.block.documentation` · `punctuation.definition.comment` · `meta.toc-list` ("Comments with special syntax that denote a section of code, should use the following scope **on the text only**. This will cause it to be shown in the symbol list.")
- **constant**: `constant.numeric` · `.integer` `.integer.binary/.octal/.decimal/.hexadecimal/.other` · `.float` `.float.binary/.octal/.decimal/.hexadecimal/.other` · `.complex` `.complex.real` `.complex.imaginary` · `constant.language` · `constant.character.escape` · `constant.other.placeholder` · `constant.other`
- **entity**: "The notable exceptions are `entity.name.tag` and `entity.other.attribute-name`, which are used in HTML and XML tags."
  `entity.name.class` `entity.name.struct` `entity.name.enum` `entity.name.union` `entity.name.trait` `entity.name.interface` `entity.name.impl` `entity.name.type` · `entity.name.class.forward-decl` ("`forward-decl` variants … can be used to exclude identifiers from the symbol list and indexing.") · `entity.other.inherited-class` · `entity.name.function` `entity.name.function.constructor` `entity.name.function.destructor` · `entity.name.namespace` · `entity.name.constant` · `entity.name.label` · `entity.name.section` (markup headings) · `entity.name.tag` ("This is the only `entity.name` scope that is applied to repeated constructs.") · `entity.other.attribute-name`
- **invalid** — verbatim, both warnings:
  &gt; "Elements that are illegal in a specific context should use the following scope. **Overuse of this will likely lead to unpleasant highlighting for users as they edit code.** `invalid.illegal`"
  &gt; "Deprecated elements should be scoped using the following scope. **This should be very rarely used, as users may be working with older versions of a language.** `invalid.deprecated`"
  (Sanctioned uses appear in syntax.html: `invalid.illegal.stray-bracket-end`, `invalid.illegal.stray-semi-colon`.)
- **keyword**: `keyword.control` `keyword.control.conditional` `keyword.control.import` · `punctuation.definition.keyword` · `keyword.other` · `keyword.operator` `.assignment` `.arithmetic` `.bitwise` `.logical` · `keyword.operator.word` ("When the operator is a word, such as `and`, `or` or `not`")
- **markup** — verbatim: "**Markup scopes are used for content, as opposed to code. This includes syntaxes such as Markdown and Textile.**"
  `markup.heading` · `markup.list.unnumbered` `markup.list.numbered` · `markup.bold` `markup.italic` `markup.underline` · `markup.inserted` `markup.deleted` · `markup.underline.link` · `markup.quote` · `markup.raw.inline` `markup.raw.block` · `markup.other` ("Other markup, including constructs such as footnotes and tables")
- **meta**: `meta.class` `meta.struct` `meta.enum` `meta.union` `meta.trait` `meta.interface` `meta.impl` `meta.type` · `meta.function` `meta.function.parameters` `meta.function.return-type` · `meta.namespace` · `meta.preprocessor` · `meta.annotation` `meta.annotation.identifier` `meta.annotation.parameters` `punctuation.definition.annotation` · `meta.path` · `meta.function-call` · `meta.block` + `punctuation.section.block.begin/.end` · `meta.braces` + `punctuation.section.braces.begin/.end` · `meta.group` + `punctuation.section.group.begin/.end` · `meta.parens` + `punctuation.section.parens.begin/.end` · `meta.brackets` + `punctuation.section.brackets.begin/.end` · `meta.generic` + `punctuation.definition.generic.begin/.end` · `meta.tag` · `meta.paragraph` ("Paragraphs in markup languages use: `meta.paragraph`") · also `meta.string`, `meta.interpolation` (in the string section)
- **punctuation** (free-standing only): `punctuation.separator` · `punctuation.terminator` · `punctuation.separator.continuation` · `punctuation.accessor`
- **source**: "A language-specific variant of the following scope is typically applied to the entirety of a source code file: `source`"
- **storage**: `storage.type` · `storage.modifier` · `storage.type.function` + `keyword.declaration.function` · `storage.type.class`/`struct`/`enum`/`union`/`trait`/`interface`/`impl`/`type` each paired with `keyword.declaration.&lt;same&gt;`. Note verbatim: "**This includes `storage.type` for backwards compatibility with older color schemes.**" — i.e. Sublime double-scopes deliberately, so old themes keep working while `keyword.declaration.*` is the semantically right name.
- **string**: `string.quoted.single/.double/.triple/.other` · `meta.string` (entirety incl. punctuation/prefixes/suffixes/interpolations) · `punctuation.definition.string.begin/.end` · `string.unquoted` · `string.regexp` · interpolation — verbatim: "the `string.*` scope should be **removed using `clear_scopes:`**, and the following should be added to the entirety of the interpolation, including punctuation: `meta.interpolation`" + `punctuation.section.interpolation.begin/.end` + "Between the punctuation, the interpolated expression should get: `source.*language-suffix*.embedded`"
- **support**: `support.constant` `support.function` `support.module` · `support.type` `support.class` ("While also used for base frameworks, many syntaxes apply these to scopes unrecognized classes and types, effectively scoping all user constructs.")
- **text** — the prose/code split at the ROOT, verbatim:
  &gt; "**Programming languages use `source.` as their base scope, whereas content uses `text.`. One of the biggest differences is the fact that many plugins and other dynamic functionality is disabled within `text.` scopes. `markup.` scopes are typically used within text.**"
  &gt; "HTML should use the following scope. **Variants for this scope are different than other scopes, in that the variant is always added after the `.html`**, such as `text.html.basic` or `text.html.markdown`." · `text.xml`
- **variable**: `variable.other` `variable.other.readwrite` · `punctuation.definition.variable` · `variable.other.constant` ("Depending on the language and semantics, `entity.name.constant` may be a better choice.") · `variable.language` · `variable.parameter` · `variable.other.member` · `variable.function` ("only when they are being invoked. When defined, they should use `entity.name.function`.") · `variable.annotation` · `punctuation.definition.annotation`

**Load-bearing for a markdown-lineage grammar:** `text.html.markdown` is the only root scope whose language variant goes *after* `.html`, not last. That breaks the otherwise-universal "language last" rule.

